course: dictionary
lesson: 11859
title: Rude words and what they tell you
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: «Bollocks» is a rude word. Grown-ups say it when they are cross or when they think something is untrue. You may hear it in films, adult books and real speech, but you will never see it in school writing. Good readers notice rude words and do not copy them.
explain: Read the words around it to find its meaning. «Dad said, "Bollocks, that's not true!" when he heard the news.» Dad thinks the news is nonsense, so bollocks means nonsense here. On its own, «Bollocks!» can be a swear word that shows someone is annoyed.
explain: Some phrases use bollocks in special ways. «Don't talk bollocks» means don't talk nonsense. «He bollocked up the model, and it fell apart» means he made a mess of it. The meaning is clear, but it is still a rude word, so ask a grown-up before you use it.
fact: «Bollocks» is a rude word, so it is not used in polite or school writing.
fact: «Bollocks» can mean nonsense or rubbish, as in «That's bollocks».
fact: «Bollocks» can be used as a swear word when you are annoyed, like «damn».
fact: In British English, «the dog's bollocks» means something excellent, but this is very rude slang.
fact: In the dictionary it is a noun, often in the plural, and it can also be a word shouted out on its own.
exercise: Mum shouted «Bollocks!» when she dropped the eggs. What does this show? | She is reading a book | She is very happy | *She is annoyed | She is fast asleep
exercise: Tom said, «That story is bollocks, dragons are not real.» What does bollocks mean here? | *Nonsense, something untrue | A long journey | A type of bird | Excellent and wonderful
exercise: Should you use «bollocks» in your school writing? | Yes, if your teacher is not looking | Yes, it is a polite word for school essays | Yes, but only in capital letters | *No, it is rude, so it does not belong in school writing
test: «The dog's bollocks» appears in an English book as slang. What does it mean? | Something very wet and muddy | *Something excellent | A dog that is lost | A type of dog
test: «Don't talk bollocks» means which of these? | Tell a long story slowly | Talk louder and faster | Talk about a dog | *Don't talk nonsense
exam: You meet a rude word in a book. What should you do? | Stop reading and never open the book again | *Read the words around it to work out the meaning, then ask a grown-up before you use it | Ask your friends to shout it out loud | Copy the word into your school writing so you remember it
